            <ArticleTitle>[Diagnostic exercise tests in adult men with no symptoms or with non-angina complaints].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>211-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to investigate clinical and ECG stress test differences in middle aged males either asymptomatic or with non-anginal symptoms and no previous myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">retrospective stress data file analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatients at the stress test laboratory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">296 consecutive patients referred to diagnostic treadmill testing from January-1988 to June-1989.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">no differences were found between symptomatic or asymptomatic individuals in the 17 variables analysed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">despite the fact that patients with chest symptoms even if obviously non-anginal have an increased coronary disease prevalence when compared with age-matched asymptomatics, this difference seems to insufficient to be demonstrated by the ECG stress test results, making questionable the practical relevance of this symptomatic classification.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
